from qb import Q, O

Q("P1-Q08", "2.1",
  "After a prompt update, the agent started calling lookup_invoice for nearly every message that mentions money, including 'I was charged for an order that never arrived', and never checks delivery status. The new system prompt contains the rule: 'For any message mentioning a charge, payment, or price, use lookup_invoice.' Tool descriptions for lookup_invoice and lookup_order are both clear and distinct. What is the best fix?",
  [O("Remove the keyword-triggered routing rule from the system prompt and let the existing tool descriptions, which state each tool's inputs and boundaries, drive selection.", True,
     "Correct. The keyword rule overrides well-written descriptions and creates an unintended association between 'charge' and one tool."),
   O("Add more keywords such as 'shipped', 'delivery', and 'tracking' to the prompt rule and map them to lookup_order.", False,
     "This extends a brittle keyword scheme. Mixed messages such as the charge-for-undelivered-order case will still match whichever rule the model weighs most."),
   O("Set tool_choice to force lookup_invoice on every turn so the behavior at least becomes predictable for billing messages.", False,
     "Forcing one tool on every turn prevents the agent from ever using lookup_order or answering, and it treats a prompt wording problem as a configuration problem."),
   O("Merge lookup_invoice and lookup_order into a single lookup_record tool so there is no routing decision to make.", False,
     "Merging hides two different backends behind one interface and discards the clear descriptions that were working. The root cause is the prompt rule.")],
  "Tool descriptions are the main mechanism the model uses to choose tools, but system prompt wording can override them. Keyword-sensitive instructions (here 'charge' maps to lookup_invoice) create unintended tool associations. The fix is to remove or rewrite the keyword rule rather than pile on more.",
  "Keyword-sensitive system prompt instructions overriding tool descriptions (Task 2.1).",
  "The detail that tool descriptions are already good and the problem began after a prompt update points to the prompt rule as the cause.",
  "After any tool-selection regression, diff the system prompt as well as the tool descriptions.")

Q("P1-Q09", "2.4",
  "Your support-engineering team uses Claude Code while developing against the company's MCP servers for get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, and escalate_to_human. Every engineer needs these servers when they clone the repository, but the CRM token must not be committed. One engineer also wants to try an experimental MCP server that only they will use. How should you configure this?",
  [O("Put the shared servers in the project's .mcp.json using ${CRM_API_TOKEN} environment variable expansion, and add the experimental server to the engineer's user-scoped ~/.claude.json.", True,
     "Correct. Project scope is shared through version control, expansion keeps secrets out of the file, and user scope keeps the experiment personal."),
   O("Ask each engineer to add all servers, including the shared ones, to their own ~/.claude.json using a token from the team password manager.", False,
     "This is not shared via version control, so engineers do not get the servers when they clone; setup must be replicated by hand and configurations drift."),
   O("Write the shared server definitions and the literal token in CLAUDE.md so Claude Code reads them at the start of each session.", False,
     "CLAUDE.md holds instructions and context, not MCP server configuration, and it would commit a secret in plain text."),
   O("Commit .mcp.json with the literal token because the repository is private, and tell the experimenting engineer to edit the same file locally.", False,
     "A private repository still exposes the secret to everyone with access and its history, and local edits to a shared file risk accidental commits.")],
  "MCP server scope matters: project-level .mcp.json is for shared team tooling and supports environment variable expansion such as ${GITHUB_TOKEN}-style references so credentials stay out of version control. User-level ~/.claude.json holds personal or experimental servers. All configured servers' tools are available simultaneously.",
  "MCP server scoping and credential management (Task 2.4).",
  "Two requirements pull in different directions: share with the team on clone, and keep one server personal. They map directly to project scope with expansion and user scope.",
  "Share the configuration, never the secret. Use environment variables for credentials.")

Q("P1-Q10", "2.3",
  "To avoid creating multiple agents, a team gave its single support agent 18 tools: the four support tools plus administrative tools such as delete_account, adjust_price_list, bulk_credit, and export_customers. In testing the agent sometimes picks a similar administrative tool for ordinary requests, and administrative actions need staff approval anyway. What is the best change?",
  [O("Give the resolution agent only the four support tools and keep administrative capabilities out of its tool set, reachable only through escalate_to_human and staff-operated tooling.", True,
     "Correct. A small, role-specific tool set improves selection and applies least privilege."),
   O("Keep all 18 tools and add a prompt paragraph explaining which tools are for staff and warning the agent not to use them.", False,
     "Large tool sets raise selection difficulty, and prompt warnings leave destructive tools callable. Compliance is probabilistic."),
   O("Set tool_choice to 'any' so the model must call one of the 18 tools on every turn, which reduces conversational mistakes.", False,
     "tool_choice 'any' only guarantees that some tool is called. It does nothing to prevent the wrong tool, and it forces calls on turns that need none."),
   O("Add a second model that reviews each tool call and vetoes administrative calls, while keeping the full tool list for the main agent.", False,
     "This adds latency and cost and relies on another probabilistic check, when removing unnecessary tools solves the problem directly.")],
  "Giving an agent far more tools than it needs degrades selection reliability and increases misuse risk. The guide describes scoping each agent to the tools it needs, with limited cross-role tools for high-frequency needs. Administrative actions that require human approval belong behind the escalation path, not in the resolution agent's tool list.",
  "Tool distribution, scoped access, and least privilege (Task 2.3).",
  "The stem says administrative tools need staff approval anyway, so the agent has no legitimate use for them. Removing them is both simpler and safer than controlling their use.",
  "The safest tool is the one the agent cannot call. Scope the tool set to the role.")

Q("P1-Q11", "2.2",
  "process_refund currently returns the string 'Operation failed' for three different situations: a gateway timeout, an order ID that does not exist, and a refund amount above the agent's authority. The agent retries all three and sometimes tells customers 'something went wrong on our side' even when the refund exceeds policy. What is the best improvement to the tool?",
  [O("Return an MCP error result (isError) with structured metadata: an error category, an isRetryable flag, and a customer-appropriate description, including non-retryable flags for policy violations.", True,
     "Correct. The agent can retry transient failures, correct validation errors, and explain or escalate business-rule failures."),
   O("Keep the generic message but add a system prompt rule telling the agent to retry any failed refund up to three times before escalating.", False,
     "Retrying a non-existent order or a policy violation cannot succeed, and the agent still cannot tell failure causes apart."),
   O("Return an empty result for all failures so that the agent treats the refund as pending and tries again later in the conversation.", False,
     "An empty successful result hides the failure and risks telling the customer a refund is coming when it is not."),
   O("Return a verbose stack trace and server log excerpt so the model can work out the cause by reading it.", False,
     "Stack traces are noisy and may leak internals. The agent needs a small, stable error contract it can act on.")],
  "The isError pattern lets MCP tools signal failure to the agent. Distinguishing transient, validation, business, and permission errors, and indicating whether each is retryable, enables the right recovery: retry timeouts, fix input errors, and explain or escalate policy failures with a clear customer-friendly message.",
  "Structured error responses with category and retryability (Task 2.2).",
  "Three different causes produce one message, so the agent applies one recovery. Only richer error metadata lets the agent choose among recoveries.",
  "Design the error contract as an API for the agent: category, retryable, human-readable explanation.")

Q("P1-Q12", "4.2",
  "To improve ambiguous-case handling, the team added eight few-shot examples to the system prompt, all of them standard damaged-item refunds with photo evidence. Now the agent also refunds cases that only look similar, such as damage reported 90 days after delivery under a 30-day policy, instead of escalating. Which change best addresses this?",
  [O("Replace them with a small set of targeted examples that straddle the decision boundary, each showing the reasoning for why the agent refunded, declined, or escalated instead of the plausible alternative.", True,
     "Correct. Contrasting examples with reasoning teach the distinction the agent is currently missing and let it generalize."),
   O("Add twenty more standard damaged-item examples so the agent sees the refund pattern more often and recognizes it with higher confidence.", False,
     "More of the same pattern strengthens the overgeneralization. The agent needs to see where the pattern stops applying."),
   O("Remove the examples and add the instruction 'when in doubt, approve the refund to keep customers satisfied'.", False,
     "That removes the escalation judgment entirely and makes the problem of unauthorized exceptions worse."),
   O("Require the agent to state a confidence score with each decision and refund automatically when the score is above 80 percent.", False,
     "Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated, and the agent is already confidently wrong on these cases.")],
  "Few-shot examples are most useful for ambiguous cases when they show the reasoning for choosing one action over plausible alternatives, and they help the model generalize judgment to novel patterns. A set that only shows the typical case teaches the model to pattern-match on surface features.",
  "Targeted few-shot examples for ambiguity (Task 4.2, 5.2).",
  "The failures are look-alike cases outside policy, which signals missing contrast in the examples rather than a lack of volume.",
  "Choose examples for the edges of the decision boundary, and include why, not just what.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q13", "4.1",
  "The refund prompt says 'Be conservative and only approve a refund when you are highly confident it is eligible.' Reviewers see inconsistent decisions on identical situations, especially whether damage 'in transit' qualifies. Select the TWO changes that most directly improve consistency.",
  [O("Replace the general instruction with explicit categorical criteria that name which situations are eligible, ineligible, and must be escalated.", True,
     "Correct. Specific categories give the model something concrete to apply and audit."),
   O("Add concrete examples of each outcome, including borderline damage cases, showing how the criteria apply.", True,
     "Correct. Examples anchor the criteria and show boundary handling."),
   O("Require the agent to approve only when it reports a confidence of at least 90 percent in its decision.", False,
     "Confidence thresholds are a vague proxy and self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated. They do not define what counts as eligible."),
   O("Tell the agent to think very carefully and double-check each decision before answering.", False,
     "This adds no new information about what the policy requires, so inconsistency is likely to persist."),
   O("Increase max_tokens so the agent has more room to reason about each refund decision.", False,
     "Output length limits are not the cause of inconsistent criteria.")],
  "General instructions such as 'be conservative' do not improve precision the way specific categorical criteria do. Defining what to approve, decline, and escalate, with concrete examples for each, gives consistent classification. Both correct choices make the standard explicit.",
  "Explicit criteria and examples instead of vague conservatism (Task 4.1).",
  "The inconsistency on identical situations shows the standard is undefined. Only options that define the standard address that.",
  "Replace adjectives like 'conservative' with testable rules and examples.", diff="I")

Q("P1-Q14", "4.1",
  "The agent's case summary panel shows three automatic flags: 'order status verified' (98% accurate), 'billing mismatch detected' (94%), and 'suspected return abuse' (about 45% accurate). Human reviewers now ignore the whole panel, including the accurate flags. Which action best restores trust while the problem is fixed?",
  [O("Temporarily stop surfacing the 'suspected return abuse' flag, route those cases to a separate review path, and refine its criteria before re-enabling it.", True,
     "Correct. Removing the noisy category protects trust in the accurate categories while the prompt is improved."),
   O("Keep all three flags but instruct the model to report only high-confidence findings so fewer false flags appear.", False,
     "A vague confidence filter does not fix the criteria that cause false positives."),
   O("Keep all three flags and add a disclaimer that some flags may be wrong, so reviewers know to verify them.", False,
     "A disclaimer does not repair the credibility loss, and reviewers will keep ignoring the panel."),
   O("Increase the number of flags shown per case so reviewers have more signals to compare against each other.", False,
     "More signals add more noise and do not address the 45% false positive category.")],
  "High false positive categories undermine trust in accurate categories. The guide recommends temporarily disabling a noisy category to restore trust while improving its prompt with explicit criteria, rather than relying on general confidence-based filtering.",
  "False positive rates and developer or reviewer trust (Task 4.1).",
  "Reviewers ignore the entire panel because of one category, so isolating that category is the fastest way to restore the value of the others.",
  "Ship only categories you can stand behind. Fix or hide the rest.")

Q("P1-Q15", "5.1",
  "A billing dispute session runs for 25 turns. lookup_order returns 40+ fields, the customer raises three issues, and the agent starts forgetting amounts and order numbers stated earlier. Select the THREE changes that best preserve critical information.",
  [O("Trim lookup_order results to the few fields relevant to the return or dispute (for example with a PostToolUse hook) before they accumulate in context.", True,
     "Correct. Verbose tool output consumes tokens disproportionately to its relevance."),
   O("Keep a persistent 'case facts' block of amounts, dates, order numbers, and statuses in every prompt, outside summarized history.", True,
     "Correct. Transactional facts are protected from summarization loss."),
   O("Store structured issue data (order ID, amount, status per issue) in a separate context layer so each concern stays distinct.", True,
     "Correct. Multi-issue sessions need per-issue structured state."),
   O("Spawn a new subagent for every tool call to hold its output so that the main agent's context never contains tool results.", False,
     "The main agent needs the relevant results to reason, and adding agents per call adds complexity without solving the loss of key facts."),
   O("Summarize the whole conversation into two sentences every three turns and discard the earlier messages.", False,
     "Aggressive progressive summarization turns precise numbers and dates into vague statements."),
   O("Keep only the last two turns and rely on the model to recall earlier details from its general knowledge.", False,
     "The model cannot recall session-specific facts it no longer has in context.")],
  "Three techniques from the guide apply: trim verbose tool outputs to relevant fields, extract transactional facts into a persistent case-facts block outside summarized history, and keep structured per-issue data in a separate layer. Progressive summarization is the very mechanism that loses numbers and dates.",
  "Context preservation in long, multi-issue sessions (Task 5.1).",
  "Forgotten amounts and order numbers are precisely what summarization tends to blur, and the 40-field responses show where tokens are wasted.",
  "Protect facts by storing them explicitly, not by hoping a summary keeps them.")

Q("P1-Q16", "5.2",
  "At the start of a chat the customer writes: 'Skip the bot. I want a human agent now.' The agent responds by calling get_customer and lookup_order 'to see if I can quickly fix it' and then offers a solution. The customer replies angrily. What should the agent have done?",
  [O("Acknowledge the request and escalate to a human right away, including a handoff summary of what is known so far, without first investigating.", True,
     "Correct. An explicit request for a human should be honored immediately."),
   O("Continue investigating first, because resolving the issue quickly is the best way to satisfy the customer's underlying goal.", False,
     "This overrides a clear customer preference. Offering resolution first is only appropriate when the customer did not demand a human."),
   O("Ask the customer to explain why they want a human, and escalate only if the reason shows the case is complex.", False,
     "Requiring a justification adds friction to an explicit request."),
   O("Run sentiment analysis and escalate only if the customer's frustration score passes a threshold.", False,
     "Sentiment is not a reliable proxy for the need to escalate, and here the customer already stated what they want.")],
  "Appropriate escalation triggers include a customer's request for a human, policy exceptions or gaps, and inability to progress. When a customer explicitly demands a human, escalate immediately. The nuance is that if a customer only expresses frustration while the issue is straightforward, the agent can acknowledge and offer resolution.",
  "Escalation triggers and honoring explicit customer requests (Task 5.2).",
  "The phrase 'I want a human agent now' is an explicit request, which differs from simple frustration. That makes immediate escalation correct.",
  "Respect explicit channel preferences. Reserve 'try to fix it first' for cases where the customer has not asked otherwise.", diff="I")

Q("P1-Q17", "5.1",
  "A developer calls the Messages API once per customer turn and sends only the newest user message plus a one-line summary. The agent keeps asking for the order number the customer already gave and repeats the same lookup_order call. The team proposes adding a subagent to hold the conversation memory. What is the better fix?",
  [O("Send the complete conversation history on every request, including prior assistant tool_use blocks and the matching tool_result blocks.", True,
     "Correct. The API is stateless, so coherence depends on passing full history."),
   O("Add a memory subagent that stores the conversation and answers the main agent's questions about what was said earlier.", False,
     "This adds an agent boundary and handoff risk to solve a problem that sending the history solves directly."),
   O("Increase the length of the one-line summary so it includes more of what the customer said in earlier turns.", False,
     "A hand-written summary is lossy and still omits tool results the agent needs for coherent reasoning."),
   O("Instruct the agent in the system prompt to always ask the customer to repeat details so nothing is assumed.", False,
     "This makes the experience worse and does not give the model the information it needs.")],
  "The Messages API does not remember earlier requests. Maintaining conversational coherence requires passing the complete history, including tool_use and tool_result blocks, in each request. A subagent is unnecessary; the problem is missing context, not missing agents.",
  "Complete conversation history in subsequent requests (Task 5.1, 1.1).",
  "Repeated questions and duplicate lookups are classic symptoms of the model not seeing prior turns.",
  "Do not add agents to compensate for missing state. Fix the state first.", diff="F")
